./logs/iter_007_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1281:With strict crossing, finite-grid prefix values can be much smaller than continuum `sup` values if the grid lacks points just below a threshold. For example, with action grid \(\{0,1/2,1\}\) and terminal threshold \(c=1/2\), the best grid quote below \(c\) is \(0\), so the grid prefix value is \(0\), while the continuum comparator supremum is \(1/2\). For \(C=\{1/2,1\}\), this finite grid gives no \(1/4\) two-threshold gap. A slightly finer but still coarse grid such as \(\{0,1/4,1/2,3/4\}\) changes the local value again. Therefore finite-grid LP duals must be stated for grid-restricted learners, or converted to continuum statements by dense-grid/\(\eta\)-comparator limits.
./logs/iter_007_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1293:- Weinberger and Ordentlich (IEEE TIT 2002) is an older fixed-delay prediction reference, now verified through DBLP/DOI metadata. Useful background for individual-sequence prediction with delayed observations; not specific to market-making fills.
./logs/iter_007_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1318:- Kleinberg and Leighton (FOCS 2003) is verified through DOI/DBLP metadata and accessible technical-report text. It is foundational posted-price regret background and gives lower-bound context for threshold/pricing geometry.
./logs/iter_007_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1332:- Yao (FOCS 1977) is verified through DOI/IEEE/DBLP metadata. It is relevant background for distributional-vs-randomized lower-bound framing, although the finite two-continuation block proof here is elementary averaging and does not require invoking the full principle.
./logs/iter_007_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1334:- Abernethy, Bartlett, and Hazan (COLT/PMLR 2011) is verified. Approachability/no-regret equivalence may help if simultaneous-prefix constraints are formulated as feasibility/approachability, but it does not prove the horizon-oblivious lower bound.
./logs/iter_007_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1335:- Hazan and Seshadhri (ICML/ACM 2009) and Daniely, Gonen, and Shalev-Shwartz (ICML/PMLR 2015) are verified adaptive/strongly-adaptive regret references. They are useful contrast for simultaneous interval/prefix guarantees, but their ordinary per-round loss setting does not contradict horizon-censored quote rewards.
./logs/iter_007_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1340:- Blackwell (Pacific J. Math. 1956) and Sion (Pacific J. Math. 1958) are verified as classical approachability/minimax references. They are relevant only as conceptual/minimax support for the prefix-compatibility LP; the market-making transfer still needs a direct proof.
./logs/iter_007_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1341:- Kwon and Perchet (AISTATS/PMLR 2017) and Kwon (JMLR 2021) are verified. They provide modern approachability/partial-monitoring/global-cost background for vector-deficit formulations, but do not directly prove the proposed \(\kappa\) modulus theorem.
./logs/iter_007_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1342:- Luo, Senapati, and Sharan (NeurIPS/OpenReview 2025) is verified. Its simultaneous-regret/calibration viewpoint is a loose analogy for simultaneous-prefix compatibility, not a delayed-feedback or market-making result.
./logs/iter_007_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1343:- Hu, Tian, and Yang (COLT 2026/arXiv:2602.17577) is verified. It develops simultaneous Blackwell approachability with coupled actions for multiclass omniprediction; useful analogy for simultaneous-prefix feasibility, not direct support for the delayed market-making lower bounds.
./logs/iter_007_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1344:- Frongillo, Luo, Mehta, and Schneider (COLT 2026/arXiv:2606.18527) is verified. It studies simultaneous regret in U-calibration; useful analogy for one forecast/action sequence serving many downstream losses, but not a delayed-feedback theorem.
./logs/iter_007_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1353:Existing delayed-learning literature supports sufficiency via bounded, total, adaptive, optimistic, skipped, outstanding-observation, or intermediate-observation delay terms. It does not support necessity of the unweighted \(\beta\) tail. Standard online-learning references also reinforce that the meaningful lower-bound regime is usually universal/minimax or simultaneous-prefix, not horizon- and law-specific oracle policies. Iteration 4's accepted concatenation theorems are direct elementary lower bounds, with the cited unknown-horizon/anytime literature serving as terminology and contrast. The literature is more aligned with aggregate, quantile, best-arm, skipped, value-of-information, horizon-censored, censored-threshold, or adaptive-regret quantities, but the counterexamples show raw delayed reward mass is not necessary for instance-wise learnability unless tied to unpredictability, prefix incompatibility, or information acquisition.
./logs/iter_007_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1355:Iteration 6 verifies that the multi-threshold \(\kappa\) LP aligns with classical posted-price/equal-revenue geometry: local gaps are welfare minus monopoly revenue and are capped by \(1/e\). The simultaneous-approachability and U-calibration sources are analogies for one action sequence satisfying several objectives. None of these external sources supplies a global delayed market-making characterization; the accepted lower bounds remain internal prefix-indistinguishability arguments.
./logs/iter_007_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1418:- Jacob Abernethy, Peter L. Bartlett, Elad Hazan. "Blackwell Approachability and No-Regret Learning are Equivalent." COLT 2011, PMLR 19:27-46. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v19/abernethy11b.html. Relevance: possible framework for simultaneous constraint feasibility or prefix-compatibility formulations.
./logs/iter_007_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1422:- Elad Hazan, C. Seshadhri. "Efficient Learning Algorithms for Changing Environments." Proceedings of the 26th International Conference on Machine Learning, 2009, pp. 393-400. DOI: https://doi.org/10.1145/1553374.1553425. Relevance: adaptive regret background for simultaneous interval/prefix quantifiers.
./logs/iter_007_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1438:- Shie Mannor, John N. Tsitsiklis, Jia Yuan Yu. "Online Learning with Sample Path Constraints." Journal of Machine Learning Research 10:569-590, 2009. https://jmlr.org/beta/papers/v10/mannor09a.html. Relevance: sample-path constraint framework that may help formulate simultaneous-prefix compatibility, but this is speculative and not proof support.
./logs/iter_007_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1446:- Haipeng Luo, Spandan Senapati, Vatsal Sharan. "Simultaneous Swap Regret Minimization via KL-Calibration." NeurIPS 2025 spotlight, OpenReview. https://openreview.net/forum?id=Ib4ZXPXpss. Relevance: simultaneous-regret/calibration analogy for multiple-prefix compatibility; not a delayed-feedback result.
./logs/iter_007_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1452:- David Blackwell. "An analog of the minimax theorem for vector payoffs." Pacific Journal of Mathematics 6(1):1-8, 1956. https://msp.org/pjm/1956/6-1/p01.xhtml. Relevance: classical origin of approachability/vector-payoff feasibility; conceptual background for prefix-compatibility formulations.
./logs/iter_007_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1454:- Maurice Sion. "On general minimax theorems." Pacific Journal of Mathematics 8(1):171-176, 1958. https://msp.org/pjm/1958/8-1/p14.xhtml. Relevance: direct minimax theorem background for finite-grid LP duality; not by itself a market-making lower-bound proof.
./logs/iter_007_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1456:- Joon Kwon, Vianney Perchet. "Online Learning and Blackwell Approachability with Partial Monitoring: Optimal Convergence Rates." AISTATS 2017, PMLR 54:604-613. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v54/kwon17a.html. Relevance: approachability with partial monitoring and multi-criteria objectives; possible framework for dynamic prefix-compatibility variants.
./logs/iter_007_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1458:- Joon Kwon. "Refined approachability algorithms and application to regret minimization with global costs." Journal of Machine Learning Research 22(200):1-38, 2021. https://jmlr.org/papers/v22/20-1019.html. Relevance: vector-payoff/global-cost regret background for compatibility-gap objectives.
./logs/iter_007_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1466:- Lunjia Hu, Kevin Tian, Chutong Yang. "Simultaneous Blackwell Approachability and Applications to Multiclass Omniprediction." COLT 2026 / arXiv:2602.17577. https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.17577. Relevance: simultaneous approachability with coupled actions; analogy for prefix-compatibility and simultaneous-horizon feasibility.
./logs/iter_007_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1468:- Rafael Frongillo, Haipeng Luo, Nishant A. Mehta, Jon Schneider. "Toward Simultaneously Optimal Regret in U-Calibration." COLT 2026 / arXiv:2606.18527. https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18527. Relevance: simultaneous-regret/U-calibration analogy for one sequence serving many downstream objectives.
