./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1571:Set \(T_j=M+N+j\). Consider one horizon-oblivious policy, possibly process-aware and randomized, whose zero-block actions \(A_1,\ldots,A_N\) are the same random variables for all evaluations \(T_j\). Regret is against comparator suprema, and each quote pays at most once.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1690:Failed completely for the problem's pseudo-regret. If a policy may depend on both the exogenous law \(P\) and the horizon \(T\), it can choose an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimal fixed quote for \(V_T^P(a)=\mathbb E_P\sum_t r_t(a)\) and play it every round. This gives \(R_T^P\le\varepsilon_T=o(T)\) for every process, so no nontrivial tail condition can be necessary under this quantifier order. This failure relies on pseudo-regret, exogeneity, and horizon/law awareness.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1698:Failed. Removing horizon awareness from the process-aware oracle regime is a real restriction. On the known deterministic path \(0^N,1/2,1\), the same early quotes must serve both prefixes \(N+1\) and \(N+2\), whose best fixed quotes approach \(1/2\) and \(1\), respectively. LB-011 shows one of the two prefix regrets is \(\Omega(N)\). Thus the horizon-aware collapse CL-014 does not automatically extend to one anytime policy.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1702:Failed. The horizon-oblivious regime is nontrivial, but sparse vanishing spikes still give \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\) and the always-zero policy is an anytime policy with \(O(1)\) regret. Any necessity theorem in this regime must measure prefix incompatibility or decision-relevant delayed information, not the unweighted uniform tail alone.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1706:Failed. Iteration 4 promotes two real lower bounds, but both depend on stronger quantifiers: a process-unaware universal learner facing a sequence chosen after the learner is fixed, or one horizon-oblivious policy evaluated at incompatible prefixes. Neither theorem says that every process with \(\beta\not\to0\) or \(\gamma\not\to0\) is hard. Process-aware horizon-specific oracle policies and sparse vanishing spikes remain decisive obstructions to any raw instance-wise tail necessity statement.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1710:Failed as a general principle. A positive local gap \(\kappa_I(\mathcal U)\) only lower-bounds regret when the policy model forces the same prefix action law across candidates and when rewards outside the tested prefix cannot compensate the local deficit. After the candidate continuation is revealed, a long suffix can let the learner recover; on rare prefix events, a large conditional gap contributes only its event probability to expected regret. Any transfer lemma must include an outside-surplus cap \(B_u\), probability weighting for the common-prefix event, and a precise definition of the candidate set as alternative laws/horizons rather than realized hidden futures.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1835:Even if the learner knows this path, a horizon-oblivious policy must choose the same first \(N\) quotes whether regret is later evaluated at \(T=N+1\) or \(T=N+2\). Quotes below \(1/2\) trade by both prefixes but under-earn at the longer prefix; quotes in \([1/2,1)\) fail at \(N+1\) but earn at \(N+2\). LB-011 gives
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1841:Concatenating dominant blocks \(0^{N_i},1/2,1\) yields a deterministic known process with no horizon-oblivious anytime \(o(T)\)-regret policy, provided \(M_i=\sum_{j<i}(N_j+2)=o(N_i)\). At paired endpoints \(T_i^0=M_i+N_i+1\) and \(T_i^1=M_i+N_i+2\),
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1859:With strict crossing, finite-grid prefix values can be much smaller than continuum `sup` values if the grid lacks points just below a threshold. For example, with action grid \(\{0,1/2,1\}\) and terminal threshold \(c=1/2\), the best grid quote below \(c\) is \(0\), so the grid prefix value is \(0\), while the continuum comparator supremum is \(1/2\). For \(C=\{1/2,1\}\), this finite grid gives no \(1/4\) two-threshold gap. A slightly finer but still coarse grid such as \(\{0,1/4,1/2,3/4\}\) changes the local value again. Therefore finite-grid LP duals must be stated for grid-restricted learners, or converted to continuum statements by dense-grid/\(\eta\)-comparator limits.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1899:if the learner is told the evaluation horizon \(T_j=M+N+j\), it can tailor its zero-block quotes to \(c_j\). The common zero-block action law across \(j\), which is the input to the \(\kappa(C)\) averaging argument, is then absent. This is the same obstruction as the horizon/process-aware oracle collapse: CL-026 and CL-027 apply only to one infinite horizon-oblivious policy.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1909:- Weinberger and Ordentlich (IEEE TIT 2002) is an older fixed-delay prediction reference, now verified through DBLP/DOI metadata. Useful background for individual-sequence prediction with delayed observations; not specific to market-making fills.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1934:- Kleinberg and Leighton (FOCS 2003) is verified through DOI/DBLP metadata and accessible technical-report text. It is foundational posted-price regret background and gives lower-bound context for threshold/pricing geometry.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1947:- Chen, Hu, and Wang (Operations Research 2024) is verified. It gives robust screening/minimax-pricing duality adjacent to the \(\kappa\) LP viewpoint.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1948:- Cummings, Devanur, Huang, and Wang (SODA 2020) is verified. It provides finite-dimensional algorithmic price-discrimination/segmentation LP context that may be useful for computational versions of \(\kappa\).
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1954:- Yao (FOCS 1977) is verified through DOI/IEEE/DBLP metadata. It is relevant background for distributional-vs-randomized lower-bound framing, although the finite two-continuation block proof here is elementary averaging and does not require invoking the full principle.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1956:- Abernethy, Bartlett, and Hazan (COLT/PMLR 2011) is verified. Approachability/no-regret equivalence may help if simultaneous-prefix constraints are formulated as feasibility/approachability, but it does not prove the horizon-oblivious lower bound.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1957:- Hazan and Seshadhri (ICML/ACM 2009) and Daniely, Gonen, and Shalev-Shwartz (ICML/PMLR 2015) are verified adaptive/strongly-adaptive regret references. They are useful contrast for simultaneous interval/prefix guarantees, but their ordinary per-round loss setting does not contradict horizon-censored quote rewards.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1962:- Blackwell (Pacific J. Math. 1956) and Sion (Pacific J. Math. 1958) are verified as classical approachability/minimax references. They are relevant only as conceptual/minimax support for the prefix-compatibility LP; the market-making transfer still needs a direct proof.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1963:- Kwon and Perchet (AISTATS/PMLR 2017) and Kwon (JMLR 2021) are verified. They provide modern approachability/partial-monitoring/global-cost background for vector-deficit formulations, but do not directly prove the proposed \(\kappa\) modulus theorem.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1964:- Luo, Senapati, and Sharan (NeurIPS/OpenReview 2025) is verified. Its simultaneous-regret/calibration viewpoint is a loose analogy for simultaneous-prefix compatibility, not a delayed-feedback or market-making result.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1965:- Hu, Tian, and Yang (COLT 2026/arXiv:2602.17577) is verified. It develops simultaneous Blackwell approachability with coupled actions for multiclass omniprediction; useful analogy for simultaneous-prefix feasibility, not direct support for the delayed market-making lower bounds.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1966:- Frongillo, Luo, Mehta, and Schneider (COLT 2026/arXiv:2606.18527) is verified. It studies simultaneous regret in U-calibration; useful analogy for one forecast/action sequence serving many downstream losses, but not a delayed-feedback theorem.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1969:- Luo, Senapati, and Sharan (NeurIPS/OpenReview 2024/arXiv:2405.19374) is verified. It gives minimax rates for multiclass U-calibration, reinforcing simultaneous-regret analogies; not direct support for delayed market making.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1979:Existing delayed-learning literature supports sufficiency via bounded, total, adaptive, optimistic, skipped, outstanding-observation, or intermediate-observation delay terms. It does not support necessity of the unweighted \(\beta\) tail. Standard online-learning references also reinforce that the meaningful lower-bound regime is usually universal/minimax or simultaneous-prefix, not horizon- and law-specific oracle policies. Iteration 4's accepted concatenation theorems are direct elementary lower bounds, with the cited unknown-horizon/anytime literature serving as terminology and contrast. The literature is more aligned with aggregate, quantile, best-arm, skipped, value-of-information, horizon-censored, censored-threshold, or adaptive-regret quantities, but the counterexamples show raw delayed reward mass is not necessary for instance-wise learnability unless tied to unpredictability, prefix incompatibility, or information acquisition.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1981:Iteration 6 verifies that the multi-threshold \(\kappa\) LP aligns with classical posted-price/equal-revenue geometry: local gaps are welfare minus monopoly revenue and are capped by \(1/e\). The simultaneous-approachability and U-calibration sources are analogies for one action sequence satisfying several objectives. None of these external sources supplies a global delayed market-making characterization; the accepted lower bounds remain internal prefix-indistinguishability arguments.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1985:Iteration 8 promotes the same-path multi-threshold anytime lift internally; no direct external theorem was found for this delayed-market-making statement. The new calibration/U-calibration sources are decision-relevant simultaneous-objective analogies only. They should be cited, if at all, to motivate replacing raw tail conditions by payoff/actionability moduli, not as proof support for CL-026/CL-027.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2048:- Jacob Abernethy, Peter L. Bartlett, Elad Hazan. "Blackwell Approachability and No-Regret Learning are Equivalent." COLT 2011, PMLR 19:27-46. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v19/abernethy11b.html. Relevance: possible framework for simultaneous constraint feasibility or prefix-compatibility formulations.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2052:- Elad Hazan, C. Seshadhri. "Efficient Learning Algorithms for Changing Environments." Proceedings of the 26th International Conference on Machine Learning, 2009, pp. 393-400. DOI: https://doi.org/10.1145/1553374.1553425. Relevance: adaptive regret background for simultaneous interval/prefix quantifiers.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2068:- Shie Mannor, John N. Tsitsiklis, Jia Yuan Yu. "Online Learning with Sample Path Constraints." Journal of Machine Learning Research 10:569-590, 2009. https://jmlr.org/beta/papers/v10/mannor09a.html. Relevance: sample-path constraint framework that may help formulate simultaneous-prefix compatibility, but this is speculative and not proof support.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2076:- Haipeng Luo, Spandan Senapati, Vatsal Sharan. "Simultaneous Swap Regret Minimization via KL-Calibration." NeurIPS 2025 spotlight, OpenReview. https://openreview.net/forum?id=Ib4ZXPXpss. Relevance: simultaneous-regret/calibration analogy for multiple-prefix compatibility; not a delayed-feedback result.
