uction","counterexample search"],"summary":"Diagonalize the finite-m block lower bound with m_i -> infinity and N_i dominating both previous length M_i and any threshold-menu overhead. The target theorem is a universal/process-unaware deterministic block class with limsup R_T/T >= 1/e - o(1), improving the binary 1/4 constant. Verify whether the same construction keeps beta and gamma tails large in the intended sense.","title":"Approach A 1/e Universal Lower Bound","why_promising":"The solver's constant tends to 1/e, matching the accepted continuum κ cap, so this may identify the sharp local minimax obstruction for universal delayed market making."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Intermediate threshold-round quotes may create extra learner rewards or comparator terms; an O(m) loss is probably safe, but removing it may require careful sup-comparator bookkeeping.","suggested_lenses":["same-path horizon-oblivious proof","strict-crossing bookkeeping"],"summary":"Prove the same-path multi-threshold lift on deterministic known blocks 0^N,c_1,...,c_m, with regret evaluated after each threshold time. Show that a horizon-oblivious policy's shared zero-block actions imply max_j R_{T_j} >= N κ(C)-M-O(m), and then specialize to equal-revenue C_m. Track exactly how quotes posted at intermediate threshold times affect both learner reward and comparator value.","title":"Multi-Threshold Anytime Prefix Conflict","why_promising":"This would strengthen the accepted known-path horizon-oblivious obstruction from the binary 1/4 case to the finite-m and possibly 1/e regime."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"A local mixed strategy need not compose into a global no-regret algorithm because actions affect many future horizons and censoring changes the payoff matrix over time.","suggested_lenses":["minimax primal","algorithm design","finite computations"],"summary":"Derive the primal optimizer for the finite-m zero-block game: find an action distribution μ minimizing sup_c [c - E_{a~μ} a 1{a<c}] for the equal-revenue threshold set, and take the m -> infinity limit. Use small finite LP computations to guess a closed form. Then ask whether this local defensive distribution can be used as a building block for upper bounds in simultaneous-prefix or universal settings.","title":"Find The Primal Equal-Revenue Action Law","why_promising":"The dual constant is valuable, but an explicit primal strategy would clarify sharpness and may point toward matching upper bounds or approachability formulations."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"The resulting condition may become tautological if it just restates regret; it needs either an algorithmic consequence or a clean lower-bound transfer theorem.","suggested_lenses":["approachability","simultaneous-prefix characterization"],"summary":"Recast the process-aware horizon-oblivious deterministic-path problem as simultaneous approximation of prefix value functions V_T^*=sup_a sum_{t<=T} r_t(a). Define vector deficits over a moving family of horizons and test whether Blackwell/approachability-style conditions characterize when one action sequence can keep all prefix regrets sublinear. Use sparse vanishing spikes as an easy positive test and multi-threshold blocks as negative tests.","title":"Known-Path Simultaneous-Prefix Framework","why_promising":"It targets the quantifier regime where oracle collapse fails but raw beta-necessity is still false, so it may be the right replacement for tail characterization."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The construction must avoid being trivialized by horizon-aware oracle policies; it should be stated for expected regret under initial law and with explicit learner information.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","rare-event weighting"],"summary":"Build a rare-prefix process where, conditional on a rare event, a long multi-threshold block has κ close to 1/e, but the event probability p_i makes p_i N_i=o(T). Show that essential-sup or conditional κ tails remain large while expected regret is small for a simple process-aware policy. This would formalize the rare-history obstruction with the sharper finite-m gadget.","title":"Rare Multi-Threshold Prefix Stress Test","why_promising":"It would clarify why any information-tail necessity must be frequency-weighted, not based on essential sup or unweighted local hardness."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"The exact finite-m constant may not appear in the literature under the same normalization, so the search can return only adjacent terminology rather than a citation.","suggested_lenses":["literature audit","terminology mapping"],"summary":"Search the price-discrimination, robust pricing, and buyer-optimal signaling literature for the exact finite-support problem: maximize E[Z]-sup_a a Pr(Z>a) over m-point laws on [0,1]. Compare the solver proof with discrete equal-revenue distributions, finite-signal buyer-optimal schemes, and minimax pricing duals; report whether the result is known, a corollary, or apparently new.","title":"Check Whether CL-023 Is Known","why_promising":"A citation or known formulation would strengthen the final writeup and prevent duplicating an existing finite-support monopoly-pricing theorem."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
