./logs/iter_004_synthesis.stderr.log:5078: - Hazan and Seshadhri (ICML/ACM 2009) and Daniely, Gonen, and Shalev-Shwartz (ICML/PMLR 2015) are verified adaptive/strongly-adaptive regret references. They are useful contrast for simultaneous interval/prefix guarantees, but their ordinary per-round loss setting does not contradict horizon-censored quote rewards.
./logs/iter_004_synthesis.stderr.log:5090:-Existing delayed-learning literature supports sufficiency via bounded, total, adaptive, optimistic, skipped, or intermediate-observation delay terms. It does not support necessity of the unweighted \(\beta\) tail. Standard online-learning references also reinforce that the meaningful lower-bound regime is usually universal/minimax or simultaneous-prefix, not horizon- and law-specific oracle policies. The literature is more aligned with aggregate, quantile, best-arm, skipped, value-of-information, horizon-censored, censored-threshold, or adaptive-regret quantities, but the iteration's counterexamples show raw delayed reward mass is not necessary for instance-wise learnability unless tied to unpredictability, prefix incompatibility, or information acquisition.
