./logs/iter_010_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:5177:The claimed solution should not stop the loop. It is a plausible strengthening of the iteration-9 known-path \(1/e\) theorem by derandomizing the equal-revenue policy, but it does not solve the original tail-characterization problem. The critic correctly identified this as a serious mismatch: the original iff remains false, and no replacement characterization of sublinear regret in terms of tail or information conditions is proved.
./logs/iter_010_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:5179:If the target were only the deterministic-policy derandomization theorem, I see no fatal flaw in the core idea. The monotone-threshold union bound plus Hoeffding plus Borel-Cantelli argument looks repairable. But it is still a proof sketch, not a fully written theorem with exact constants, summable failure probabilities, and final quantifiers.
./logs/iter_010_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:5183:- Horizon-oblivious deterministic policy, with one fixed quote sequence independent of evaluation horizon.
./logs/iter_010_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:5265:The lower bound already covers deterministic policies because it applies even to randomized horizon-oblivious policies, but the final theorem statement should restate the exact policy class and quantifier order.
./logs/iter_010_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:5297:However, it is not yet an acceptable final solution as written. It is a proof sketch for a new deterministic-policy strengthening of the known-path \(1/e\) theorem, not a solution to the original tail-characterization problem. It also needs exact quantifiers and a fully written probabilistic-method lemma.
./logs/iter_010_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:5307:- The policy is horizon-oblivious and deterministic, with a fixed action sequence independent of the evaluation horizon.
./logs/iter_010_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:5324:- “The lower bound already covers deterministic policies because it applies even to randomized policies” is correct only because the accepted lower bound is a same-path lower bound against every horizon-oblivious policy. The writeup should restate that quantifier, not rely on memory.
./logs/iter_010_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:5362:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Noga Alon and Joel H. Spencer","relevance":"Standard reference for proving existence of deterministic objects by random construction, concentration, union bounds, and derandomization ideas relevant to the solver's monotone-threshold lemma.","title":"The Probabilistic Method","url":"https://www.wiley.com/en-be/The%2BProbabilistic%2BMethod%2C%2B4th%2BEdition-p-9781119062073","venue_year":"Wiley, 4th ed., 2016"},{"authors":"Aad W. van der Vaart and Jon A. Wellner","relevance":"Primary empirical-process reference for bracketing and uniform laws; useful for replacing the ad hoc grid union bound by a monotone-class entropy proof.","title":"Weak Convergence and Empirical Processes","url":"https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4757-2545-2","venue_year":"Springer Series in Statistics, 1996"},{"authors":"Fuchang Gao and Jon A. Wellner","relevance":"Gives metric and bracketing entropy estimates for bounded monotone function classes; relevant if the monotone-threshold derandomization is recast as an empirical-process theorem.","title":"Entropy Estimate For High Dimensional Monotonic Functions","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0512641","venue_year":"arXiv:math/0512641, 2005"},{"authors":"Harald Niederreiter","relevance":"Classical source on deterministic low-discrepancy sequences and quasi-Monte Carlo, relevant to making the nonconstructive equal-revenue sequence explicit or proving ordinary 1D discrepancy is insufficient.","title":"Random Number Generation and Quasi-Monte Carlo Methods","url":"https://epubs.siam.org/doi/book/10.1137/1.9781611970081","venue_year":"SIAM CBMS-NSF Regional Conference Series, 1992"},{"authors":"L. Kuipers and H. Niederreiter","relevance":"Classical reference for uniform distribution and discrepancy of deterministic sequences, useful for explicit sequence or discrepancy-rate variants of the solver's derandomization idea.","title":"Uniform Distribution of Sequences","url":"https://store.doverpublications.com/products/9780486149998","venue_year":"Wiley, 1974; Dover reprint"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The proof could fail through a quantifier slip in the Borel-Cantelli diagonalization, a non-summable failure probability, or strict-crossing boundary cases at c=1 and a_t=c_t. These look fixable but should be audited line by line.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","quantifier audit","strict-crossing audit"],"summary":"Turn the solver's monotone-threshold derandomization into a precise lemma: for the equal-revenue law mu on [e^{-1},1], prove that there is a deterministic sequence a_t such that uniformly over every n and every nonincreasing c_1 >= ... >= c_n, sum_t a_t 1{a_t<c_t} >= sum_t g(c_t) - e_n with e_n=o(n), preferably e_n=O(n^{2/3} sqrt(log n)). Check the grid count, one-sided Hoeffding bound, downward rounding, and Borel-Cantelli construction of one infinite realization.","title":"Audit The Monotone Derandomization Lemma","why_promising":"This directly validates or breaks the solver attempt. If true, it removes randomization from the known-path 1/e upper bound with minimal new machinery."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Standard empirical-process theorems usually assume iid observations, while here the time coordinates are fixed and the threshold sequence is triangular. The monotone-boundary class may need a custom bracketing argument rather than direct citation.","suggested_lenses":["empirical process","entropy bounds","rate sharpening"],"summary":"Reformulate the derandomization lemma as a uniform law of large numbers for the class f_c(t,a)=a 1{a<c_t}, where c_t is nonincreasing in t. Try to prove the needed uniform convergence via bracketing entropy for monotone functions or bounded-variation classes, and compare the resulting rate to the solver's finite-grid union bound.","title":"Empirical-Process Reformulation","why_promising":"A reusable entropy proof would be cleaner than an ad hoc grid argument and may also support infinite-menu or nonstationary variants later."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Ordinary one-dimensional low discrepancy may only control fixed thresholds on prefixes, while the hard class allows many time-varying monotone thresholds. An explicit van der Corput or Kronecker sequence may fail unless interval-threshold discrepancy is controlled.","suggested_lenses":["constructive derandomization","discrepancy theory","counterexample search"],"summary":"Look for a constructive replacement for the probabilistic-method sequence, for example a transformed low-discrepancy sequence a_t=exp(u_t-1), a greedy conditional-expectation construction over finite grid events, or an online discrepancy method. If explicit sequences fail, produce a monotone-threshold counterexample showing which discrepancy notion is actually needed.","title":"Constructive Deterministic Sequence","why_promising":"The current candidate upper bound is nonconstructive. An explicit policy would make the theorem algorithmic and clarify whether standard low-discrepancy tools are strong enough."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"The statement can easily mix incompatible quantifier orders: process-aware versus process-unaware policies, deterministic versus randomized policies, pathwise versus expected regret, and exogenous deterministic versus stochastic prices. The lower bound must be checked to apply to deterministic policies as a subset of randomized policies.","suggested_lenses":["theorem synthesis","quantifier audit","lower-upper matching"],"summary":"Combine the derandomized upper bound with CL-027 to state the exact deterministic known-path theorem: there exists one deterministic horizon-oblivious process-unaware policy with limsup_T R_T(P)/T <= 1/e for every deterministic exogenous path P, while there exists a deterministic path P* on which every horizon-oblivious policy has limsup_T R_T/T >= 1/e. Then add the exogenous stochastic extension by conditioning on realized paths if the pathwise proof supports it.","title":"Exact Deterministic 1/e Theorem","why_promising":"This would upgrade CL-029 from a randomized expected-regret theorem to a sharp deterministic-policy theorem, resolving the main open item named after iteration 9."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The best local distributions may depend on the whole future path and may use left-limit atoms near thresholds. The uniform derandomization becomes nonidentically distributed, and infinite suffix menus still need approximation.","suggested_lenses":["upper-bound framework","infinite-menu approximation","path-dependent characterization"],"summary":"Extend the solver's derandomization method to the path-dependent suffix-menu framework. For a known deterministic path with suffix menus C_t={M_{t,T}:T>t}, choose time-dependent distributions mu_t that achieve the local deficits delta(C_t), then prove a deterministic sequence with R_T <= sum_{t<T} delta(C_t)+o(T) uniformly over horizons. Start with finite C_t, then add an infinite-menu approximation lemma.","title":"Path-Dependent Suffix-Menu Upper Bound","why_promising":"The universal 1/e policy is worst-case sharp but loose on easy paths. This direction could move from a worst-case constant to a genuine known-path anytime characterization."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"This is not necessary for the asymptotic 1/e constant and could become a standalone discrepancy-theory problem. It may consume effort without changing the main theorem.","suggested_lenses":["finite-time rates","discrepancy lower bounds","counterexample search"],"summary":"Study the optimal finite-n error e_n in the monotone-threshold lemma. Prove lower bounds for every deterministic sequence and upper bounds via random construction, empirical-process chaining, or explicit discrepancy sequences. Determine whether the solver's O(n^{2/3} sqrt(log n)) rate is an artifact or near-optimal for this monotone-threshold class.","title":"Finite-Time Discrepancy Of Monotone Thresholds","why_promising":"A rate theorem would strengthen the deterministic upper bound and clarify the exact complexity of serving all horizon-induced monotone suffix-max sequences."}],"solution_status":"maybe"}
