./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:1965:Concatenating dominant blocks \(0^{N_i},1/2,1\) yields a deterministic known process with no horizon-oblivious anytime \(o(T)\)-regret policy, provided \(M_i=\sum_{j<i}(N_j+2)=o(N_i)\). At paired endpoints \(T_i^0=M_i+N_i+1\) and \(T_i^1=M_i+N_i+2\),
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:1983:With strict crossing, finite-grid prefix values can be much smaller than continuum `sup` values if the grid lacks points just below a threshold. For example, with action grid \(\{0,1/2,1\}\) and terminal threshold \(c=1/2\), the best grid quote below \(c\) is \(0\), so the grid prefix value is \(0\), while the continuum comparator supremum is \(1/2\). For \(C=\{1/2,1\}\), this finite grid gives no \(1/4\) two-threshold gap. A slightly finer but still coarse grid such as \(\{0,1/4,1/2,3/4\}\) changes the local value again. Therefore finite-grid LP duals must be stated for grid-restricted learners, or converted to continuum statements by dense-grid/\(\eta\)-comparator limits.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2023:if the learner is told the evaluation horizon \(T_j=M+N+j\), it can tailor its zero-block quotes to \(c_j\). The common zero-block action law across \(j\), which is the input to the \(\kappa(C)\) averaging argument, is then absent. This is the same obstruction as the horizon/process-aware oracle collapse: CL-026 and CL-027 apply only to one infinite horizon-oblivious policy.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2029:The universal equal-revenue policy is worst-case sharp but can be very loose on easy known paths. On a path \(0^N,c\) with \(c<1/e\), the universal policy gets zero from zero-block quotes until the terminal price and loses about \(Nc\), while a process-aware horizon-oblivious policy that quotes just below \(c\) has essentially zero regret on that path.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2031:The proof also relies on exogenous prices. If a future price can be chosen as a function of the learner's realized quote \(A_t\), then \(M_{t,T}\) is no longer a fixed scalar independent of \(A_t\), and the identity \(\mathbb E[r_t(A_t)\mid P]=g_\mu(M_{t,T})\) may fail. Deterministic-policy existence is now handled by CL-031, but explicit computability/cycling remains a separate question.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2037:The iteration-10 deterministic upper bound is an existence theorem for one fixed horizon-oblivious quote sequence. It is not an explicit computable policy unless a low-discrepancy or conditional-expectation construction is later supplied. It also gives a worst-case limsup constant, not a path-dependent characterization: on easy paths such as \(0^N,c\) with \(c<e^{-1}\), the equal-revenue-type sequence may lose order \(Nc\), while a process-aware policy tailored to the path can do essentially better.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2073:- Weinberger and Ordentlich (IEEE TIT 2002) is an older fixed-delay prediction reference, now verified through DBLP/DOI metadata. Useful background for individual-sequence prediction with delayed observations; not specific to market-making fills.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2098:- Kleinberg and Leighton (FOCS 2003) is verified through DOI/DBLP metadata and accessible technical-report text. It is foundational posted-price regret background and gives lower-bound context for threshold/pricing geometry.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2112:- Chen, Hu, and Wang (Operations Research 2024) is verified. It gives robust screening/minimax-pricing duality adjacent to the \(\kappa\) LP viewpoint.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2114:- Cummings, Devanur, Huang, and Wang (SODA 2020) is verified. It provides finite-dimensional algorithmic price-discrimination/segmentation LP context that may be useful for computational versions of \(\kappa\).
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2120:- Yao (FOCS 1977) is verified through DOI/IEEE/DBLP metadata. It is relevant background for distributional-vs-randomized lower-bound framing, although the finite two-continuation block proof here is elementary averaging and does not require invoking the full principle.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2122:- Abernethy, Bartlett, and Hazan (COLT/PMLR 2011) is verified. Approachability/no-regret equivalence may help if simultaneous-prefix constraints are formulated as feasibility/approachability, but it does not prove the horizon-oblivious lower bound.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2123:- Hazan and Seshadhri (ICML/ACM 2009) and Daniely, Gonen, and Shalev-Shwartz (ICML/PMLR 2015) are verified adaptive/strongly-adaptive regret references. They are useful contrast for simultaneous interval/prefix guarantees, but their ordinary per-round loss setting does not contradict horizon-censored quote rewards.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2128:- Blackwell (Pacific J. Math. 1956) and Sion (Pacific J. Math. 1958) are verified as classical approachability/minimax references. They are relevant only as conceptual/minimax support for the prefix-compatibility LP; the market-making transfer still needs a direct proof.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2129:- Kwon and Perchet (AISTATS/PMLR 2017) and Kwon (JMLR 2021) are verified. They provide modern approachability/partial-monitoring/global-cost background for vector-deficit formulations, but do not directly prove the proposed \(\kappa\) modulus theorem.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2130:- Luo, Senapati, and Sharan (NeurIPS/OpenReview 2025) is verified. Its simultaneous-regret/calibration viewpoint is a loose analogy for simultaneous-prefix compatibility, not a delayed-feedback or market-making result.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2131:- Hu, Tian, and Yang (COLT 2026/arXiv:2602.17577) is verified. It develops simultaneous Blackwell approachability with coupled actions for multiclass omniprediction; useful analogy for simultaneous-prefix feasibility, not direct support for the delayed market-making lower bounds.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2132:- Frongillo, Luo, Mehta, and Schneider (COLT 2026/arXiv:2606.18527) is verified. It studies simultaneous regret in U-calibration; useful analogy for one forecast/action sequence serving many downstream losses, but not a delayed-feedback theorem.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2133:- Anagnostides, Farina, Fishelson, Luo, and Schneider (arXiv:2602.06264, 2026) is verified. It uses response-based approachability for swap-regret minimization. Weak analogy only for future vector-payoff/simultaneous-regret formulations; the iteration-9 \(1/e\) upper bound is elementary and does not need this source.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2136:- Luo, Senapati, and Sharan (NeurIPS/OpenReview 2024/arXiv:2405.19374) is verified. It gives minimax rates for multiclass U-calibration, reinforcing simultaneous-regret analogies; not direct support for delayed market making.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2155:Existing delayed-learning literature supports sufficiency via bounded, total, adaptive, optimistic, skipped, outstanding-observation, or intermediate-observation delay terms. It does not support necessity of the unweighted \(\beta\) tail. Standard online-learning references also reinforce that the meaningful lower-bound regime is usually universal/minimax or simultaneous-prefix, not horizon- and law-specific oracle policies. Iteration 4's accepted concatenation theorems are direct elementary lower bounds, with the cited unknown-horizon/anytime literature serving as terminology and contrast. The literature is more aligned with aggregate, quantile, best-arm, skipped, value-of-information, horizon-censored, censored-threshold, or adaptive-regret quantities, but the counterexamples show raw delayed reward mass is not necessary for instance-wise learnability unless tied to unpredictability, prefix incompatibility, or information acquisition.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2157:Iteration 6 verifies that the multi-threshold \(\kappa\) LP aligns with classical posted-price/equal-revenue geometry: local gaps are welfare minus monopoly revenue and are capped by \(1/e\). The simultaneous-approachability and U-calibration sources are analogies for one action sequence satisfying several objectives. None of these external sources supplies a global delayed market-making characterization; the accepted lower bounds remain internal prefix-indistinguishability arguments.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2161:Iteration 8 promotes the same-path multi-threshold anytime lift internally; no direct external theorem was found for this delayed-market-making statement. The new calibration/U-calibration sources are decision-relevant simultaneous-objective analogies only. They should be cited, if at all, to motivate replacing raw tail conditions by payoff/actionability moduli, not as proof support for CL-026/CL-027.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2163:Iteration 9 adds two internal results: the exact finite-\(C\) local root formula \(\kappa(C)=\delta_*(C)\), and the universal equal-revenue \(1/e\) upper bound for exogenous horizon-oblivious paths. A narrow literature check found robust/minimax-pricing analogies but no direct source for the exact root formula or the delayed-market-making \(1/e\) theorem. Treat Bergemann-Schlag 2011 and Caldentey-Liu-Lobel 2017 as adjacent context only, and Anagnostides et al. 2026 as a weak approachability analogy only.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2228:Define the policy model carefully: process-aware, current-time-aware, no terminal-round signal, and one action rule shared across evaluation horizons.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2230:This is the cleanest separation between horizon-aware oracle collapse and horizon-oblivious anytime learning. It does not rescue \(\beta\)-necessity; it points instead to simultaneous-prefix compatibility.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2241:with no finite \(O(m)\) loss: intermediate threshold-round learner rewards cancel against the comparator's extra threshold-round earning opportunities. Equal-revenue menus with \(m_i\to\infty\) and \(M_i+m_i=o(N_i)\) yield a deterministic known path with \(\limsup_T R_T/T\ge1/e\) for every horizon-oblivious policy.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2249:Candidate formal objects: conditional value-of-information gaps, Le Cam/two-continuation moduli, argmax-stability gaps for \(\sup_a V_T(a)\), or simultaneous-prefix incompatibility moduli. Test any proposed modulus against both accepted block obstructions and CE-001 sparse vanishing spikes. Avoid tautological quantities unless they produce an algorithm or a clean lower bound.
./logs/iter_011_synthesis.stderr.log:2251:The most concrete current object is the prefix-compatibility gap \(\kappa_I(\mathcal U)\). Iteration 6 promoted the conditional transfer lemma and finite-grid/continuum LP dual as local tools. Next steps: write them as polished propositions, keep action domains explicit, use dense-grid/\(\eta\)-comparator limits for strict thresholds, and apply them only when same-prefix action laws, outside-surplus caps \(B_u\), and rare-event probability weights are present.
